/**
\defgroup bijector_coupled Coupled bijectors
\brief Coupled bijections between constrained vectors or matrices and Euclidean space, their inverses, log-Jacobians, and derivatives to order 6.

Functions in this module are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution. Include `<laplace/bijector/alr.hpp>`, `<laplace/bijector/log_cholesky.hpp>`, `<laplace/bijector/partial_corr.hpp>`, or `<laplace/bijector/matrix_log.hpp>`; each includes `<laplace/bijector/coupled.hpp>`. Every map is a lightweight parameter-free struct in `laplace::bijector` constructed from runtime `dim`: `alr_bijector(K)`, `log_cholesky_bijector(p)`, `partial_corr_bijector(p)`, and `matrix_log_bijector(p)`. Across orders 0 to 6, `alr_bijector` stays within 93.9 ulp, `log_cholesky_bijector` within 1.03 ulpN (inverse) and 1984 ulpN (forward), `partial_corr_bijector` within 2839 ulpN (inverse) and 7361 ulpN (forward), and `matrix_log_bijector` within 210 ulpN on the maps.

## Definitions and conventions

A coupled bijector maps a constrained set—the open \f$K\f$-simplex, symmetric positive definite \f$p \times p\f$ matrices \f$\Sigma\f$, or \f$p \times p\f$ correlation matrices \f$R\f$—one to one onto \f$\mathbb{R}^n\f$. Following \ref bijector_scalar "the scalar bijectors", forward \f$g(v)\f$ maps constrained \f$v\f$ to \f$u \in \mathbb{R}^n\f$, and inverse \f$h(u) = g^{-1}(u)\f$ returns \f$v\f$.

Because simplex sums and correlation diagonals are constrained, forward derivatives and the log-Jacobians \f$\log\lvert\det J_g(v)\rvert\f$ and \f$\log\lvert\det J_h(u)\rvert\f$ use open reference coordinates with 0-based indices. For \ref laplace::bijector::alr_bijector "alr_bijector", \f$v = \pi \in \mathbb{R}^K\f$ stores all \f$K\f$ probabilities, while reference coordinates are \f$\pi_0, \dots, \pi_{K-2}\f$ (\f$\pi_{K-1} = 1 - \sum_{b=0}^{K-2}\pi_b\f$). Symmetric matrices \f$\Sigma\f$, \f$R\f$, and Cholesky factors \f$L\f$ use row-major packed lower-triangular storage (\ref pdmat), entry \f$(i, j)\f$ (\f$0 \le j \le i < p\f$) at \f$i(i + 1)/2 + j\f$; moving \f$(i, j)\f$ (\f$i > j\f$) moves both symmetric entries. For \ref laplace::bijector::partial_corr_bijector "partial_corr_bijector", \f$u\f$ and reference coordinates use strict lower-triangular packing, entry \f$(a, b)\f$ (\f$0 \le b < a < p\f$) at \f$a(a - 1)/2 + b\f$.

Each struct stores `dim` and provides `free_size()` (\f$n\f$), `value_size()` (\f$m\f$), and `scratch_size()` (caller scratch doubles `w`, no dynamic allocation; `matrix_log_bijector` uses \f$O(p^4)\f$, 23360 doubles at \f$p = 4\f$). Order-0 functions are `forward()`, `inverse()`, `forward_log_jacobian()`, and `inverse_log_jacobian()`, plus `inverse_log()` on `alr_bijector` and `inverse_cholesky()` (\f$M = L L^\top\f$) on `log_cholesky_bijector` and `partial_corr_bijector`. For order \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ and sorted `idx` (\f$i_1 \le \dots \le i_k\f$), `forward_dk<k>()`, `inverse_dk<k>()`, and `inverse_cholesky_dk<k>()` return `false` without touching the output when structurally zero, while `forward_log_jacobian_dk<k>()` and `inverse_log_jacobian_dk<k>()` return `double` (`0.0` when zero). Bases \ref laplace::bijector::coupled_bijector_orders "coupled_bijector_orders" and \ref laplace::bijector::cholesky_bijector_orders "cholesky_bijector_orders" expose named wrappers `*_d1()`–`*_d6()`.

| Map struct | Constrained set | Reference coordinates | \f$n\f$ | \f$m\f$ | Cholesky |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `alr_bijector` | Open \f$K\f$-simplex | \f$\pi_0, \dots, \pi_{K-2}\f$ | \f$K - 1\f$ | \f$K\f$ | No |
| `log_cholesky_bijector` | Positive definite \f$\Sigma\f$ | Lower triangle of \f$\Sigma\f$ | \f$p(p + 1)/2\f$ | \f$p(p + 1)/2\f$ | Yes |
| `partial_corr_bijector` | Correlation \f$R\f$ (\f$R_{ii} = 1\f$) | Strict lower triangle of \f$R\f$ | \f$p(p - 1)/2\f$ | \f$p(p + 1)/2\f$ | Yes |
| `matrix_log_bijector` | Positive definite \f$\Sigma\f$ | Lower triangle of \f$\Sigma\f$ | \f$p(p + 1)/2\f$ | \f$p(p + 1)/2\f$ | No |

## Domain and preconditions

Dimensions, domains, and sorted indices \f$0 \le i_1 \le \dots \le i_k < n\f$ are unchecked preconditions. `alr_bijector` requires \f$K \ge 2\f$ and \f$\pi\f$ on the open simplex (\f$\pi_a > 0\f$, \f$\sum_a \pi_a = 1\f$); matrix bijectors require \f$p \ge 1\f$, positive definite \f$\Sigma\f$, and positive definite correlation \f$R\f$. At \f$p = 1\f$, `partial_corr_bijector` has \f$n = 0\f$, \f$m = 1\f$, writes \f$R_{00} = 1\f$, and returns \f$0.0\f$ for log-Jacobians.

`alr_bijector`, `matrix_log_bijector`, and forward derivatives of `log_cholesky_bijector` and `partial_corr_bijector` are dense (`true`). At order 6 for \f$p = 4\f$ (\f$K = 4\f$), nonzero inverse tuples number 10 of 5005 for `log_cholesky_bijector` (16 nonzero components of 50050), 251 of 462 for `partial_corr_bijector`, and 28 of 28 for `alr_bijector`.

## Mathematical definitions and methods

### Additive log-ratio

\ref laplace::bijector::alr_bijector "alr_bijector" maps the open \f$K\f$-simplex onto \f$\mathbb{R}^{K-1}\f$ (Aitchison, 1986). Forward evaluates \f$u_a = \log\pi_a - \log\pi_{K-1}\f$ (\f$a = 0, \dots, K - 2\f$) using \f$\pi_{K-1}\f$ as given, with \f$\log\lvert\det J_g(v)\rvert = -\sum_{a=0}^{K-1}\log\pi_a\f$. Inverse computes \f$\pi_a = \exp(u_a + l)\f$ and \f$\pi_{K-1} = \exp(l)\f$, factoring \f$u_{\max} = \max_b u_b\f$ in \f$l = \log\pi_{K-1}\f$:
\f[
l = \begin{cases}
-\operatorname{log1p}\left(\sum_{b=0}^{K-2} e^{u_b}\right) & \text{if } u_{\max} \le 0, \\
-\left[u_{\max} + \operatorname{log1p}\left(e^{-u_{\max}} + \sum_{b \ne \max} e^{u_b - u_{\max}}\right)\right] & \text{if } u_{\max} > 0.
\end{cases}
\f]
`inverse_log()` writes \f$u_a + l\f$ and \f$l\f$ without underflow; \f$\log\lvert\det J_h(u)\rvert = \sum_{a=0}^{K-2} u_a + K l\f$.

Along reference tuple \f$t = (t_1, \dots, t_k)\f$, closed forward derivatives are
\f[
\frac{\partial^k u_a}{\partial\pi_{t_1}\cdots\partial\pi_{t_k}} = [t = (a, \dots, a)] (-1)^{k-1}\frac{(k-1)!}{\pi_a^k} + \frac{(k-1)!}{\pi_{K-1}^k},
\qquad
\frac{\partial^k\log\lvert\det J_g(v)\rvert}{\partial\pi_{t_1}\cdots\partial\pi_{t_k}} = [t = (a, \dots, a)] (-1)^k\frac{(k-1)!}{\pi_a^k} + \frac{(k-1)!}{\pi_{K-1}^k},
\f]
factoring opposite-sign differences as \f$\frac{\pi_a - \pi_{K-1}}{\pi_a \pi_{K-1}}\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\pi_a^{-(k-1-j)}\pi_{K-1}^{-j}\f$. Inverse derivatives recurse over tuple subsets \f$A \subseteq \{0, \dots, k - 1\}\f$ with lowest bit \f$j\f$, \f$B = A \setminus \{j\}\f$, and category \f$\mathrm{cat}(j)\f$:
\f[
\partial_A l = -\partial_B \pi_{\mathrm{cat}(j)},
\qquad
\partial_A \pi_c = \sum_{C \subseteq B} \partial_C \pi_c \left(\partial_{A \setminus C} l + [A \setminus C = \{j\}, \mathrm{cat}(j) = c]\right),
\f]
summing \f$1 - \pi_c = \sum_{a \ne c}\pi_a\f$ to avoid cancellation near \f$\pi_c \approx 1\f$, with \f$\partial_A \pi_c = \pi_c\partial_A e^l / e^l\f$ outside the tuple. Inverse log-Jacobian derivatives equal \f$1 - K\pi_{t_1}\f$ (\f$k = 1\f$) and \f$K\partial_t l\f$ (\f$k \ge 2\f$).

### Log-Cholesky

\ref laplace::bijector::log_cholesky_bijector "log_cholesky_bijector" maps positive definite \f$\Sigma = L L^\top\f$ (\f$L_{ii} > 0\f$) via \f$u_{ii} = \log L_{ii}\f$ and \f$u_{ij} = L_{ij}\f$ (\f$i > j\f$), with \f$\log\lvert\det J_g(v)\rvert = -\big(p\log 2 + \sum_{i=0}^{p-1}(p - i + 1)\log L_{ii}\big)\f$ (Pinheiro and Bates, 1996). Inverse sets \f$L_{ii} = \exp(u_{ii})\f$, \f$L_{ij} = u_{ij}\f$, \f$\Sigma_{ij} = \sum_{q \le j} L_{iq} L_{jq}\f$, and \f$\log\lvert\det J_h(u)\rvert = p\log 2 + \sum_{i=0}^{p-1}(p - i + 1)u_{ii}\f$.

Inverse derivatives vanish unless the tuple touches at most two variables in one column \f$c\f$. A single variable \f$(r, c)\f$ of multiplicity \f$k\f$ reaches \f$\Sigma_{rr}\f$ and \f$\Sigma_{r, r'}\f$ (\f$r' \ge c\f$): for \f$r = c\f$, derivatives are \f$2^k e^{2 u_{rr}}\f$ and \f$e^{u_{rr}} L_{r', c}\f$; for \f$r > c\f$, nonzero only at \f$k \le 2\f$ on \f$\Sigma_{rr}\f$ (\f$2u_{rc}\f$ then \f$2\f$) and \f$k = 1\f$ on \f$\Sigma_{r, r'}\f$ (\f$L_{r', c}\f$). A pair \f$(r_1, c)\f$, \f$(r_2, c)\f$ (multiplicity 1 if off-diagonal) affects only \f$\Sigma_{r_1, r_2}\f$ with \f$f_1 f_2\f$ (\f$f_\nu = e^{u_{r_\nu c}}\f$ if \f$r_\nu = c\f$, else 1). Inverse log-Jacobian derivatives are \f$p - i + 1\f$ at \f$k = 1\f$ on \f$(i, i)\f$ and 0 otherwise; \f$L(u)\f$ derivatives are nonzero only on pure tuples (\f$e^{u_{ii}}\f$ on diagonal, 1 off-diagonal at \f$k = 1\f$). Forward derivatives differentiate \f$L_{jj}^2 = \Sigma_{jj} - \sum_{q < j} L_{jq}^2\f$, \f$L_{ij} L_{jj} = \Sigma_{ij} - \sum_{q < j} L_{iq} L_{jq}\f$, and \f$L_{ii}\,\partial y_i = \partial L_{ii}\f$ (\f$y_i = \log L_{ii}\f$) over subsets \f$A\f$ via Leibniz's rule, giving forward log-Jacobian derivative \f$-\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}(p - i + 1)y_i[A_{\mathrm{full}}]\f$.

### Partial correlations

\ref laplace::bijector::partial_corr_bijector "partial_corr_bijector" maps correlation matrices \f$R\f$ via canonical partial correlations \f$z_{ab} = \tanh u_{ab}\f$ (Joe, 2006; Lewandowski and others, 2009). `inverse_cholesky()` constructs \f$L\f$ without \f$1 - z_{ab}^2\f$:
\f[
L_{ab} = \tanh(u_{ab})\prod_{q<b}\operatorname{sech}(u_{aq}) \quad (a > b),
\qquad
L_{aa} = \prod_{q<a}\operatorname{sech}(u_{aq}),
\f]
giving \f$R = L L^\top\f$ (\f$R_{aa} = 1\f$) and \f$\log\lvert\det J_h(u)\rvert = -\sum_{a>b}(p - b)\log\cosh u_{ab}\f$. Forward computes \f$Q_{ab}^2 = \sum_{q=b+1}^a L_{aq}^2\f$ and \f$u_{ab} = \operatorname{asinh}(L_{ab}/Q_{ab})\f$ (\f$a > b\f$) from unit-diagonal \f$R = L L^\top\f$, with \f$\log\lvert\det J_g(v)\rvert = \sum_{a>b}(p - b)\log r_{ab}\f$ (\f$r_{ab} = \sqrt{1 + (L_{ab}/Q_{ab})^2}\f$).

Inverse derivatives multiply \f$\tanh^{(k)}\f$ and \f$\operatorname{sech}^{(k)} u = \operatorname{sech}(u) P_k\f$ along rows, with SymPy 1.14.0 polynomials \f$P_k\f$ (Meurer and others, 2017) in \f$T^2\f$ (\f$T^2 < 1/2\f$) or \f$\operatorname{sech}^2 u\f$ beyond (\f$T = \tanh u\f$). Derivatives of \f$R\f$ vanish beyond two rows: on \f$i_0 < i_1\f$, only \f$R_{i_1, i_0}\f$ is nonzero when all tuple columns in \f$i_1\f$ are \f$\le i_0\f$; on row \f$a\f$, derivatives affect \f$R_{aj}\f$ (\f$j \ne a\f$). Inverse Cholesky derivatives require one row; inverse log-Jacobian derivatives are \f$-(p - b)\tanh^{(k-1)}(u_{ab})\f$ on pure tuples and 0 otherwise. Forward derivatives recurse over subsets \f$A\f$ for unit-diagonal \f$L\f$, \f$Q_{ab}\f$, \f$w_{ab} = L_{ab}/Q_{ab}\f$, \f$r_{ab}\f$, \f$r_{ab}\,\partial u_{ab} = \partial w_{ab}\f$, and \f$\sum_{a>b}(p - b)\log r_{ab}[A_{\mathrm{full}}]\f$.

### Matrix logarithm

\ref laplace::bijector::matrix_log_bijector "matrix_log_bijector" maps positive definite \f$\Sigma\f$ via \f$S = \log\Sigma\f$ (Chiu and others, 1996). Cyclic Jacobi eigendecomposition (Forsythe and Henrici, 1960) evaluates \f$S = V\operatorname{diag}(\log\sigma)V^\top\f$ (forward) and \f$\Sigma = \exp(S) = V\operatorname{diag}(\exp(s))V^\top\f$ (inverse). Log-Jacobians are \f$\sum_{i\le j}\log f[s_i, s_j]\f$ (inverse) and \f$-\sum_{i\le j}\log f[\log\sigma_i, \log\sigma_j]\f$ (forward), where \f$f[x, y] = e^{\max(x, y)}\operatorname{exprel}(-|x - y|)\f$ (\ref laplace::math::exprel "exprel()").

Derivatives use truncated Taylor arithmetic: each distinct coordinate \f$\alpha\f$ (multiplicity \f$m_\alpha\f$) gets nilpotent \f$\epsilon_\alpha\f$ (\f$\epsilon_\alpha^{m_\alpha+1} = 0\f$), storing one \f$p \times p\f$ matrix per monomial \f$\prod_\alpha \epsilon_\alpha^{\gamma_\alpha}\f$, multiplying in \f$\prod_\alpha (m_\alpha+1)(m_\alpha+2)/2\f$ matrix products (\f$3^k p^3\f$ for \f$k\f$ distinct coordinates), and scaling the top monomial by \f$\prod_\alpha m_\alpha!\f$. Inverse evaluates \f$\exp(S + \sum_\alpha \epsilon_\alpha E_\alpha)\f$ by scaling and squaring (Higham, 2009); forward solves \f$X = \log(\Sigma + \sum_\alpha \epsilon_\alpha E_\alpha)\f$ via \f$k\f$ Newton steps \f$X \mathrel{+}= \mathcal{L}^{-1}[\Sigma(\epsilon) - \exp(X)]\f$ in the eigenbasis (Higham, 2008). Log-Jacobians evaluate \f$\log\det J(S(\epsilon))\f$ in the eigenbasis of \f$S_0\f$ using one column symbol \f$d\f$ (\f$d^2 = 0\f$) and pivoted LU in scalar Taylor arithmetic.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for metrics and bounds. Coupled errors use normwise `ulpN` (\ref accuracy_mixed): order-0 log-Jacobians scale by \f$\max(|\mathrm{ref}|, 1.0)\f$, mixed tuples by the geometric mean of pure derivatives, and vanishing derivatives by the largest derivative of their order (or nearest nonvanishing lower order). `tools/reference/bijector_coupled.py` writes `bijector_coupled.csv` using SymPy/mpmath (mpmath) at 300 bits and mixed central differences at 1200 bits.

Maximum errors over orders 0 to 6 on gcc/OpenMP and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), with the largest per-order test bound across orders 0 to 6 in `tests/bijector/test_coupled.cpp` (`ulpN` for maps and \f$L(u)\f$, `ulp` for log-Jacobians):

| Map | Quantity | gcc | CUDA | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `alr` | Inverse | 90.5 | 55.2 | 136 |
| `alr` | Inverse log-Jacobian | 90.5 | 93.9 | 141 |
| `alr` | Forward | 3.8 | 3.8 | 6 |
| `alr` | Forward log-Jacobian | 3.4 | 3.4 | 6 |
| `log_cholesky` | Inverse | 1.03 | 0.95 | 2 |
| `log_cholesky` | Inverse log-Jacobian | 0.90 (exact for \f$k \ge 1\f$) | 0.90 (exact for \f$k \ge 1\f$) | 2 |
| `log_cholesky` | Cholesky \f$L(u)\f$ | 0.44 | 0.44 | 1 |
| `log_cholesky` | Forward | 1984 | 1982 | 2976 |
| `log_cholesky` | Forward log-Jacobian | 1984 | 1982 | 2976 |
| `partial_corr` | Inverse | 2839 (\f$k = 5\f$) | 366 (\f$k = 5\f$) | 4258 |
| `partial_corr` | Inverse log-Jacobian | 2839 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 1959 (\f$k = 5\f$) | 4258 |
| `partial_corr` | Cholesky \f$L(u)\f$ | 6.2 | 4.0 | 10 |
| `partial_corr` | Forward | 7305 | 7304 | 10959 |
| `partial_corr` | Forward log-Jacobian | 7356 | 7361 | 11041 |
| `matrix_log` | Inverse | 12.0 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 12.0 (\f$k = 3\f$) | 19 |
| `matrix_log` | Inverse log-Jacobian | 102794 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 291832 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 437749 |
| `matrix_log` | Forward | 199 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 210 (\f$k = 6\f$) | 316 |
| `matrix_log` | Forward log-Jacobian | 3293 (\f$k = 3\f$) | 2364 (\f$k = 3\f$) | 4939 |

`partial_corr_bijector` inverse errors at orders 5–6 occur in \f$\tanh^{(k)}\f$ near a polynomial root (\f$u = -1.57\f$). Forward Cholesky derivatives amplify pivot error by \f$k\f$: `partial_corr_bijector` scales roughly as \f$0.3 k\operatorname{cond}(R)\f$ ulp (7305 at \f$\operatorname{cond}(R) = 2.4 \times 10^4\f$, at most 21 for \f$\operatorname{cond}(R) \le 64\f$), while `log_cholesky_bijector` stays below 2000 ulpN up to \f$\operatorname{cond}(\Sigma) = 3.4 \times 10^9\f$. `matrix_log_bijector` loses about 30 ulpN per Newton step on spread points, and order-6 log-Jacobians cancel among LU pivot logarithms where exact values are \f$1/24\f$ to \f$1/70\f$ of the order's maximum (absolute error \f$\approx 10^{-14}\f$). See `docs/numerics.md` for full records.

## Execution times

Order-0 maps and closed inverses of `alr_bijector`, `log_cholesky_bijector`, and `partial_corr_bijector` take 19 to 525 ns on one gcc thread, 5.0 to 75 ns on 8 OpenMP threads, and 1.1 to 7.9 ns on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), while `alr_bijector` order-6 inverse takes 616 to 704 ns on one thread and 31 to 33 ns on CUDA. Order-6 forward Cholesky derivatives take 3.8 to 49 \f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$ on one thread and 261 to 3446 ns on CUDA across all 64 subsets. On pure tuples of `matrix_log_bijector` (\f$p = 3, 4\f$), order-0 maps take 361 to 886 ns on one thread and 52 to 128 ns on CUDA, while order-6 derivatives take 8.0 to 612 \f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$ on one thread and 1.7 to 188 \f$\mu\mathrm{s}\f$ on CUDA.

## Parallel evaluation in Kokkos

Each thread passes one row of a 2D `Kokkos::LayoutRight` View as scratch:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/bijector/log_cholesky.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

constexpr int p = 3, n = p * (p + 1) / 2, num_items = 1000;
const laplace::bijector::log_cholesky_bijector b(p);
Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutRight> scratch("scratch", num_items, b.scratch_size());
Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutRight> u("u", num_items, n), sigma("sigma", num_items, n), dsigma("dsigma", num_items, n);
Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_log_cholesky", num_items, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
  const laplace::bijector::log_cholesky_bijector bij(p);
  bij.inverse(&u(i, 0), &sigma(i, 0), &scratch(i, 0));
  const int idx[1] = {0};
  bij.inverse_d1(&u(i, 0), idx, &dsigma(i, 0), &scratch(i, 0));
});
\endcode

## Public classes and bases

- \ref laplace::bijector::alr_bijector "alr_bijector": Additive log-ratio bijector on the probability simplex.
- \ref laplace::bijector::log_cholesky_bijector "log_cholesky_bijector": Log-Cholesky bijector on positive definite matrices.
- \ref laplace::bijector::partial_corr_bijector "partial_corr_bijector": Partial correlation bijector on correlation matrices.
- \ref laplace::bijector::matrix_log_bijector "matrix_log_bijector": Matrix logarithm bijector on positive definite matrices.
- \ref laplace::bijector::coupled_bijector_orders "coupled_bijector_orders": CRTP base adding `forward_d1()`–`forward_d6()`, `inverse_d1()`–`inverse_d6()`, `forward_log_jacobian_d1()`–`forward_log_jacobian_d6()`, and `inverse_log_jacobian_d1()`–`inverse_log_jacobian_d6()`.
- \ref laplace::bijector::cholesky_bijector_orders "cholesky_bijector_orders": CRTP base adding `inverse_cholesky_d1()`–`inverse_cholesky_d6()`.

